\begin{abstract}
Image and image--text embeddings underpin search, recommendation, retrieval and classification, yet their evaluation is fragmented across narrow, task-specific protocols. We introduce MIEB, the Massive Image Embedding Benchmark, which evaluates embeddings directly (zero-shot wherever possible) on 130 tasks in 8 categories and 38 languages, covering image-only, text-only and interleaved inputs. Beyond retrieval, classification, clustering, compositionality and vision-centric QA, MIEB introduces Visual STS, which renders textual similarity tasks as images to probe visual text understanding, and document-understanding retrieval. We evaluate 50 models spanning self-supervised vision encoders, CLIP-style encoders and MLLM-based embedding models. No single model dominates: CLIP-style encoders lead on linear probing, zero-shot classification and clustering, while MLLM-based embeddings lead on document understanding, Visual STS and multilingual retrieval. Visual STS performance of encoders correlates strongly with MLLM performance on OCR-heavy benchmarks. Finally, we derive MIEB-lite, a 51-task subset that agrees with the full benchmark (Spearman 0.992) at 70--80\% lower compute.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Image and image--text embeddings are a basic building block of search, recommendation, retrieval and classification systems. Their evaluation, however, is fragmented: common protocols focus on narrow slices of capability, such as zero-shot classification on ImageNet~\cite{deng2009imagenet}, image--text retrieval in specific domains, or linear probing, so it is hard to tell whether a model that excels at one setting (e.g., clustering) also performs well in others (e.g., multilingual retrieval or document understanding). There is consequently little consensus on what a ``universal'' image or image--text embedding model is. Multimodal LLMs (MLLMs) complicate matters further by shifting attention to downstream generative performance as a proxy for representation quality; such evaluation is computationally intensive and entangles the LLM choice, instruction tuning and data size.

We address this with MIEB, a unified, reproducible benchmark that tests embeddings directly across complementary capabilities, in the spirit of text-embedding benchmarks~\cite{muennighoff2023mteb,enevoldsen2025mmtebmassivemultilingualtext}. MIEB (\autoref{fig:mieb_tasks}, \autoref{tab:MIEB big tasks}) contains 130 tasks in 8 categories and 38 languages, and supports interleaved inputs. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item A unified benchmark of 130 tasks (8 categories, 38 languages), with modality combinations i-i, i-t, t-i, it-i, it-t, i-it, t-it and it-it, plus multilingual and instruction-aware retrieval under one standardized protocol.
\item Visual STS, a novel evaluation of visual text understanding obtained by rendering STS tasks into images, and a set of document-understanding retrieval tasks.
\item A comprehensive evaluation of 50 models, including vision-only, CLIP-style and MLLM-based embedders, with category-wise diagnostics.
\item MIEB-lite, a 51-task subset built from inter-task correlations that preserves model ranking (Spearman 0.992) and cuts compute by up to 82.4\%.
\end{itemize}

