\section{Discussions}
CLIP-style encoders excel at linear probing, zero-shot classification, clustering and many retrieval tasks, especially with scale and high-quality data (DataComp, OpenCLIP, EVA-CLIP), but lag on interleaved retrieval, OCR-heavy visual text understanding and multilingual tasks. MLLM-based embeddings excel where visual--text fusion and OCR are needed (document understanding, Visual STS, instruction-aware and multilingual retrieval). Compositionality remains hard: Winoground is particularly challenging due to image/text confounders, and reasoning or test-time scaling may be needed.

\paragraph{Limitations.} MIEB covers images and image--text only; video and audio are out of scope. Visual STS depends on rendering choices and font/layout variability; harder settings (noisy scans, handwriting) are future work. Document understanding is evaluated as retrieval, and generative or structured extraction tasks would capture complementary skills. Non-native interleaving via embedding summation may under-represent the potential of encoder-only models. Some domains, e.g., 3D/spatial reasoning, are lightly represented in vision-centric QA and compositionality.

\section{Related Work}
Text-embedding benchmarks such as BEIR~\cite{thakur2021beir}, MTEB~\cite{muennighoff2023mteb} and MMTEB~\cite{enevoldsen2025mmtebmassivemultilingualtext} established unified evaluation of embeddings; MIEB extends this idea to images. Image--text retrieval and universal multimodal retrieval were studied in UniIR~\cite{wei2023uniir}, and document retrieval in ColPali/ViDoRe~\cite{faysse2024colpali}. Vision-language pretraining with contrastive objectives~\cite{radford2021learning,jia2021scaling,zhai2023sigmoid} and MLLM-based embedders~\cite{jiang2024e5,jiang2024vlm2vec} motivate evaluating the representations themselves, rather than generative proxies such as Cambrian~\cite{tong2024cambrian}. Compositionality has been probed by Winoground~\cite{Thrush_2022_CVPR} and SugarCrepe~\cite{hsieh2023sugarcrepe}.

\section{Conclusion}
MIEB is a comprehensive, practical benchmark for image and image--text embeddings: 130 tasks, 8 categories, 38 languages, with 50 models evaluated. It reveals clear trade-offs: CLIP-style encoders excel at fine-grained vision tasks, while MLLM-based embeddings lead on multilingual, interleaved and OCR-heavy tasks. Visual STS offers an efficient route to selecting vision encoders for MLLMs, and MIEB-lite makes large-scale evaluation affordable.

